WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.568
On September 6, the Ukrainian army
launched an attack on the town of Balakliya.

00:00:04.568 --> 00:00:07.257
It had been held by the Russian army 
for more than 6 months.

00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:10.882
But by September 8, the Ukrainian army
had recaptured it.

00:00:10.882 --> 00:00:12.599
And they were just getting started.

00:00:13.276 --> 00:00:16.970
Over the next few days, Ukrainian forces
swept through this Russian-held territory

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.395
with astounding speed.

00:00:18.395 --> 00:00:21.496
"Ukrainian forces have blitzed 
through Russia's gains..."

00:00:21.496 --> 00:00:23.135
"...in a lightning counter-offensive..."

00:00:23.135 --> 00:00:25.914
"...forcing Russian forces to retreat..."

00:00:26.266 --> 00:00:29.253
As of September 14, Ukraine claims it recaptured

00:00:29.253 --> 00:00:32.487
nearly 8,000 square kilometers of 
Russian-held territory

00:00:32.487 --> 00:00:34.883
and liberated dozens of towns.

00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:39.020
It’s a major breakthrough for Ukraine and
it marks a new phase in the war.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.281
So, how did Ukraine pull off such
a stunning attack?

00:00:42.281 --> 00:00:44.138
And how does it change the war?

00:00:53.226 --> 00:00:57.305
It’s useful to think of the war in Ukraine
as having three main phases, so far.

00:00:57.305 --> 00:01:00.449
The first began when Russia invaded
in February 2022.

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:03.800
The Russian military swept into Ukraine through
the north, east, and south

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.293
and immediately clashed with 
the Ukrainian army.

00:01:06.293 --> 00:01:10.090
This phase was essentially the Russians 
attempting to force

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.830
the Ukrainian government to surrender.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.960
This is Mason Clark, a Russia analyst

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.944
at the Institute for the Study of War,
an American think tank.

00:01:16.944 --> 00:01:21.162
Ukrainian forces at the time were
in large part outgunned.

00:01:21.162 --> 00:01:24.000
The most intense fighting took place
in a few major cities

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.225
like Kharkiv, Mariupol, Kherson, and
outside the capital, Kyiv.

00:01:28.225 --> 00:01:30.549
But by April, a few weeks into the war

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:33.000
Russia had failed to capture Kyiv or Kharkiv.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.429
It did control a large swath
of territory, though

00:01:35.429 --> 00:01:38.793
and continued to fight the Ukrainian army
on three main fronts:

00:01:38.793 --> 00:01:43.395
In the northeast, in the east, and in the
south, where it did control Kherson.

00:01:43.395 --> 00:01:45.332
That's when the second phase began.

00:01:49.274 --> 00:01:52.259
From about April to August, this map
changed very little.

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:55.850
Russia, for the most part, stopped conducting
large, sweeping attacks

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:59.366
and instead relied on its artillery to pound
Ukrainian positions.

00:01:59.366 --> 00:02:01.635
But the Russian army was in bad shape.

00:02:01.635 --> 00:02:04.991
The US government estimates that 
as many as 80,000 Russian soldiers

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:06.216
have been killed or wounded.

00:02:06.216 --> 00:02:08.420
It was very, very devastating for them

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.780
particularly since the losses
they were taking early in the war

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.480
were in the the best of the Russian military.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.480
The easy comparison is

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.580
by this summer, the Russians had lost
more men than the Soviets did

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.826
in nearly a decade of the war in Afghanistan.

00:02:23.826 --> 00:02:27.360
So, during the summer, Russia used the time
to recruit more soldiers.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.970
But it didn't go well.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.840
A fascinating element of this is that

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.550
the Kremlin still is calling this
a special military operation.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.537
It cannot be referred to as a war.

00:02:36.537 --> 00:02:41.381
That actually matters because the Russians
haven't called up conscripts for this invasion.

00:02:41.381 --> 00:02:45.730
And instead, they're trying to fill these
gaps with these various ad hoc methods.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.849
The Russians have had to hire more mercenaries.

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:49.630
And offer payments to prisoners.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:53.503
They’ve even raised the age limit
to allow men over 40 to join the army.

00:02:53.503 --> 00:02:57.101
And Russia’s sent many of these men,
with little training, to the front lines.

00:02:57.101 --> 00:03:01.397
So it's led to a steady drop off in the quality
of Russian personnel throughout the war.

00:03:01.397 --> 00:03:05.500
But while Russia struggled to rebuild its army, 
Ukraine had the opposite problem.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.630
There was actually a sort of 
bottleneck in May

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:12.620
where they had too many recruits and
not enough instructors to train them

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.285
which is a very good problem to have.

00:03:15.285 --> 00:03:16.930
With help from its western allies

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.212
Ukraine was able to quickly train lots of
these men

00:03:19.212 --> 00:03:20.553
before sending them to the front.

00:03:20.553 --> 00:03:25.430
That has really helped the Ukrainian military
not just produce more soldiers

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.290
but very effective soldiers, better than
the Russians.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.441
By the end of August they were ready for the
next phase of the war.

00:03:31.441 --> 00:03:32.480
Their own attack.

00:03:33.293 --> 00:03:35.290
Throughout the summer, Ukrainian leaders 
talked a lot

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.361
about their plans to launch an attack.

00:03:37.361 --> 00:03:43.320
And they made it pretty clear that they would
strike here, Kherson, key to control the south.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.155
Ukraine moved many of its troops into position
near the city.

00:03:46.155 --> 00:03:49.540
And used their most-advanced artillery 
to hit the Russians for weeks.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.531
They were forcing the under-manned 
Russian army to make a choice:

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:56.090
they had to pick somewhere to send forces
and they picked Kherson.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.010
When the Russians moved troops to Kherson,
they left the northeast vulnerable

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.314
since it was mostly guarded by
those experienced soldiers.

00:04:02.314 --> 00:04:05.345
So when Ukraine did start their attack 
in early September

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:06.926
they didn’t hit much resistance there.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.813
Almost as soon as Ukraine’s soldiers began
the attack, the Russian forces began to flee.

00:04:16.813 --> 00:04:21.130
These are piles of weapons and ammunition,
left behind by Russian troops near Kharkiv.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.707
They also left trucks, tanks, 
and artillery behind.

00:04:23.707 --> 00:04:26.321
And this is a Russian tank crew 
frantically fleeing

00:04:26.321 --> 00:04:28.120
before crashing into a tree.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.479
The Russians were running away and withdrawing

00:04:30.479 --> 00:04:33.163
faster than the Ukrainians were
keeping up with them.

00:04:33.163 --> 00:04:34.520
While the Russians retreated

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.452
Ukraine took over all this territory

00:04:36.452 --> 00:04:38.943
which included some important towns.

00:04:38.943 --> 00:04:40.896
Izyum was a Russian supply dump.

00:04:40.896 --> 00:04:45.329
And Kupiansk was a crucial railway hub,
useful for moving troops and supplies.

00:04:45.329 --> 00:04:47.319
They’ve also freed thousands 
of Ukrainian civilians

00:04:47.319 --> 00:04:49.649
that have been trapped under 
Russian occupation for months.

00:04:49.649 --> 00:04:53.129
I think this was a very well done,
opportunistic attack

00:04:53.129 --> 00:04:56.650
that went just incredibly well for
the Ukrainian military.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:00.780
Meanwhile, Ukraine is still attacking Kherson
and making some progress.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.099
But the attack in Kharkiv has begun
a third phase in the war

00:05:04.099 --> 00:05:06.428
where Ukraine, this time, has the momentum.

00:05:06.428 --> 00:05:08.134
There's a long road ahead.

00:05:08.134 --> 00:05:12.710
The Russians are going to try and replace
these further catastrophic losses

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:14.805
with likely diminishing returns.

00:05:14.805 --> 00:05:18.490
But I am confident in saying that
the shoe is more on the other foot now

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:22.129
with Ukrainian forces having the initiative
of choosing where

00:05:22.129 --> 00:05:24.259
the next major battle of the war will occur.

00:05:24.259 --> 00:05:25.360
And they have clearly demonstrated

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.349
that they can and fully intend
to take this war into 2023

00:05:29.349 --> 00:05:31.876
and drive out the occupying Russian forces.

